```cpp
template <typename T>
void print(T val) {
    // Generic implementation
    std::cout << val << std::endl;
}

// Explicit specialization for const char*
template <>
void print<const char*>(const char* val) {
    std::cout << "C-string: " << val << std::endl;
}
```
The `template <>` syntax indicates a full specialization, and `print<const char*>` specifies the type for which it's specialized.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Counter {
public:
    static int count; // Declaration
    Counter() { count++; }
    ~Counter() { count--; }
};

// Definition/Initialization of the static member for each T
template <typename T>
int Counter<T>::count = 0;

// Example usage:
// Counter<int> int_c1; // Counter<int>::count is 1
// Counter<double> double_c1; // Counter<double>::count is 1
// Counter<int> int_c2; // Counter<int>::count is 2
```
Each specialization of the class template (`Counter<int>`, `Counter<double>`) gets its *own distinct* `static` member `count`.

Non-type template parameters allow values (like integers, pointers, or lvalue references) instead of types to be passed as template arguments. They must be compile-time constants.
```cpp
template <typename T, int N> // N is a non-type template parameter
class FixedArray {
public:
    T arr[N]; // Array of size N
    T& operator[](int i) { return arr[i]; }
    const T& operator[](int i) const { return arr[i]; }
    int size() const { return N; }
};

// Instantiation example:
FixedArray<int, 10> intArray; // An array of 10 ints
FixedArray<double, 5> doubleArray; // An array of 5 doubles
```
